58455 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cc546b2a-f18f-5a02-9cc9-a4dfb2de1de2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558470 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558470 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558470 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186558211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558470 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e9189462-1f98-510b-b2f5-d3d5e93103dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558512 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c0f5df5-2d8b-5cef-a048-d87044456178" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558512 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-t)={e}^{-t} \\\\ r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t} \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-t}}{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-t}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334302" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558553 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a83f3a56-7f37-59d6-8ed6-dd0cc81608ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558553 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558572 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558572 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558572 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558572 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558572 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558572 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558572 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558572 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2ad2fef-a91c-56d1-9362-f2fbd4a52e56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558572 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(2t)={e}^{2t} \\\\ r(5t)={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t} \\\\ 5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}}{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{5{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{5t}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2t\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}}{3t}+\\frac{5{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{5t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t} \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5t\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}}{3t}+\\frac{5{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{5t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558595 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558595 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20da1ed7-1f0c-5835-8b4d-dcc25234e0b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558595 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558602 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "28175361-001a-5814-a2f8-5b17cada3b66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558512 #edb/ref 1759218604541